**user**:
> I have a string in Julia of the following form:
> "Name: MarketFeatureCache\n\nDescription: Preprocesses high/low price p..."
> 
> That is, a variable-like name and a multi-line description. How can I extract out this information?
**assistant**:
> ```julia
> s = "Name: MarketFeatureCache\n\nDescription: Preprocesses high/low price p..."
> 
> m = match(r"^Name:\s*(.+?)\s*\n\s*\nDescription:\s*(.*)$"s, s)
> 
> name = m.captures[1]
> description = m.captures[2]
> 
> # name == "MarketFeatureCache"
> # description == "Preprocesses high/low price p..."
> ```
> 
> The `s` flag makes `.` also match newlines, so this works for multi-line descriptions.
> 
> For a slightly more robust reusable function:
> 
> ```julia
> function parse_named_description(s::AbstractString)
>     m = match(r"^Name:\s*(.+?)\s*\n\s*\nDescription:\s*(.*)$"s, s)
>     isnothing(m) && error("Expected Name and Description fields")
>     return (; name = m.captures[1], description = m.captures[2])
> end
> ```
> 
> Then:
> 
> ```julia
> info = parse_named_description(s)
> info.name
> info.description
> ```
**user**:
> I have another string which has a bunch of other stuff in it, but the first line is meant to be a dependencies list, behind a Julia comment mark. For example:
> "# Dependencies: MarketFeatureCache\n..."
> How can I extract that? I used the match approach you provided before.
**assistant**:
> ```julia
> s = "# Dependencies: MarketFeatureCache\nother stuff\n..."
> 
> m = match(r"^#\s*Dependencies:\s*(.*)$"m, s)
> deps = strip(m.captures[1])
> 
> # deps == "MarketFeatureCache"
> ```
> 
> The `m` flag makes `^` and `$` operate line-by-line, so this finds the dependencies comment even if it is not literally the first line.
> 
> If it must be the first line, omit `m`:
> 
> ```julia
> m = match(r"^#\s*Dependencies:\s*(.*)$", s)
> ```
> 
> For comma-separated dependencies:
> 
> ```julia
> deps_list = filter(!isempty, strip.(split(deps, ',')))
> ```
